course: dictionary
lesson: 14647
title: Words that make things happen
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe an action that makes something else happen. Precipitate is one of them. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in books, news and history more often than in everyday chat. Look at the words around it for clues: what happened first, and what came after.
explain: «The shouting precipitated a fight in the playground.» The shouting came first and the fight came next, so precipitated must mean made it happen suddenly. Test your meaning by putting it in the sentence: «The shouting made a fight happen suddenly» still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Precipitate can also be an adjective meaning done too quickly, without thinking. «Mia made a precipitate choice and ran out of the door.» The word choice and the quick running tell you she did not stop to think. Hasty means much the same thing here.
explain: In science, precipitate is a chemistry word. «When the two clear liquids were mixed, a white precipitate formed at the bottom.» The new solid that separates out of the liquid is called a precipitate, so scientists use this word in their lessons.
fact: Precipitate is a verb meaning to make something happen suddenly or sooner than expected.
fact: Precipitate can also be an adjective meaning done too quickly, without thinking.
fact: In chemistry, a precipitate is a solid that forms and separates out of a liquid during a reaction.
fact: «Precipitation» is the word for rain, snow, sleet and hail that falls from clouds.
fact: Context helps: look at what happened before and after the word to work out what it means.
exercise: «The loud bang precipitated a stampede of frightened horses.» What does precipitated mean here? | Quietly explained it | Painted it over | Stopped it from happening | *Made it happen suddenly
exercise: «She made a precipitate decision and jumped off the bridge without looking.» What does precipitate mean here? | Very heavy and slow | *Done too quickly, without thinking | Happy and calm | Carefully planned for a whole week
exercise: In chemistry, what is a precipitate? | *A solid that forms and separates out of a liquid during a reaction | A gas that bubbles away | A clear liquid that stays on top | A type of cloud
test: «The argument precipitated a crisis in the town.» What does precipitated suggest? | *A bad situation was made to happen suddenly | A new park was built | Everyone agreed calmly | Something was slowly forgotten
test: Which word is the best synonym for precipitate in «a precipitate decision»? | Careful | *Hasty | Slow | Quiet
exam: Which word means rain, snow, sleet and hail falling from the sky? | *Precipitation | Evaporation | Precipitate | Condensation
